-- Q2543: Property Transactions: Basis [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Treasury Regulation Section 1.1012-1(a), when multiple assets are acquired in a lump-sum purchase, the total cost must be allocated among the individual assets in proportion to their relative fair market values at the acquisition date. This ensures each asset receives an appropriate depreciable or amortizable basis for tax purposes.
Wrong (A): Assigning the entire cost to the asset with the longest useful life ignores the required FMV-based allocation and would distort depreciation deductions.
Wrong (B): Dividing cost equally among all assets has no basis in tax law and would misstate each asset''s depreciable basis.
Wrong (C): Using the seller''s historical book values does not reflect current market conditions and is not the required allocation method.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2543;

-- Q2546: Property Transactions: Basis [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treasury Regulation Section 1.165-9(b)(2), when property is converted from personal to business use, the basis for determining a loss is the lower of adjusted basis or fair market value at the date of conversion. This prevents taxpayers from deducting losses attributable to personal-use decline in value.
Wrong (A): Using the original cost ignores the FMV cap at conversion and would allow deduction of personal-use losses.
Wrong (C): Using FMV regardless of original cost is the rule only when FMV is lower than adjusted basis; if FMV exceeds basis, the adjusted basis controls.
Wrong (D): Adding capital improvements to original cost describes adjusted basis, but the loss basis is still capped at FMV at conversion when FMV is lower.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2546;

-- Q2547: Property Transactions: Basis [easy/L1]
