-- Q2558: Property Transactions: Basis [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1015(a), the donee''s basis is generally the donor''s adjusted basis ($5,000). Per Section 1015(d)(6), basis is increased by the portion of gift tax attributable to net appreciation: $800 x ($7,000 / $12,000) = $467. James''s basis is $5,000 + $467 = $5,467.
Wrong (A): $5,000 is the donor''s unadjusted basis and ignores the gift tax adjustment required by Section 1015(d)(6) when gift tax is paid.
Wrong (B): $5,800 incorrectly adds the entire $800 gift tax to the donor''s basis instead of only the portion attributable to net appreciation ($7,000 / $12,000).
Wrong (D): $12,000 is the FMV at the date of gift. Unlike inherited property under Section 1014, gifted property does not receive a full step-up to FMV.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2558;

-- Q2559: Property Transactions: Basis [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 1014 governs property acquired from a decedent, providing an FMV basis (step-up or step-down) at the date of death. Section 1015 governs property acquired by gift, providing a carryover basis from the donor with a dual-basis rule for loss property. These are the primary basis rules for gratuitous transfers.
Wrong (B): This reverses the two sections. Section 1014 applies to inherited property, not gifted property, and Section 1015 applies to gifts, not inheritances.
Wrong (C): Both sections apply to all types of property (real, personal, tangible, intangible), not only real property.
Wrong (D): Neither section inherently provides a "lower" or "higher" basis. Section 1014 uses FMV (which may be higher or lower than cost), and Section 1015 carries over the donor''s basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2559;

-- Q2561: Property Transactions: Basis [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 1014(b)(9), only the decedent''s share of jointly held property receives a stepped-up basis. In a common law state, Beth''s half retains her original basis of $100,000, while her husband''s half receives a step-up to $250,000 (half of $500,000 FMV). Total basis: $100,000 + $250,000 = $350,000.
Wrong (B): $200,000 is the original purchase price and ignores the step-up on the decedent''s half entirely.
Wrong (C): $500,000 assumes a full step-up on both halves, which applies only in community property states under Section 1014(b)(6), not common law states.
Wrong (D): $250,000 represents only the stepped-up value of the decedent''s half and omits Beth''s own $100,000 basis in her half.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2561;

-- Q2562: Property Transactions: Basis [easy/L2]
