-- Q2573: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 1211(b), individual taxpayers may deduct up to $3,000 of net capital losses against ordinary income annually. The $12,000 STCL nets against the $5,000 LTCG under Section 1222(6), producing a $7,000 net short-term capital loss. Of this, $3,000 is deductible and $4,000 carries forward under Section 1212(b) retaining its short-term character.
Wrong (B): The net loss retains its short-term character because the short-term losses exceed the long-term gains. It is not recharacterized as long-term.
Wrong (C): $12,000 ignores the netting of the $5,000 LTCG against the STCL. Gains and losses must be netted before applying the $3,000 deduction limit.
Wrong (D): Capital losses are not treated as ordinary losses and cannot be fully deducted. The $3,000 annual limitation under Section 1211(b) applies.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2573;

-- Q2574: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 1250 recapture applies only to the excess of accelerated depreciation over what straight-line depreciation would have been. Since MACRS requires straight-line for real property (39-year nonresidential), there is typically no Section 1250 ordinary recapture. However, unrecaptured Section 1250 gain (total straight-line depreciation) is taxed at a maximum 25% rate under Section 1(h)(1)(E).
Wrong (A): Recapturing all depreciation regardless of method describes Section 1245 (personal property), not Section 1250 (real property).
Wrong (B): Limiting recapture to the year of sale has no basis in the Code. Section 1250 looks at the cumulative excess of accelerated over straight-line depreciation.
Wrong (C): Limiting recapture to the first year''s depreciation is arbitrary and not supported by Section 1250 or any other recapture provision.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2574;

-- Q2575: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Paul''s total gain is $400,000 - $280,000 = $120,000. Since all depreciation was straight-line, there is no Section 1250 ordinary recapture. However, under Section 1(h)(1)(E), unrecaptured Section 1250 gain equals the lesser of total gain ($120,000) or depreciation claimed ($120,000). The entire $120,000 is unrecaptured Section 1250 gain taxed at a maximum 25% rate.
Wrong (A): $0 incorrectly assumes no unrecaptured gain exists. Although there is no Section 1250 ordinary recapture, the straight-line depreciation is still subject to the 25% rate.
Wrong (B): $40,000 does not correspond to any correct calculation of gain or depreciation in this scenario.
Wrong (C): $80,000 understates the unrecaptured gain. The full $120,000 of depreciation is recaptured because the total gain equals or exceeds the depreciation claimed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2575;

-- Q2576: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): The Section 1231 lookback rule requires net Section 1231 gains to be recharacterized as ordinary income to the extent of unrecaptured net Section 1231 losses from the five preceding tax years. Marcus has $15,000 in prior unrecaptured losses, so $15,000 of his $40,000 gain is ordinary income and the remaining $25,000 is long-term capital gain.
Wrong (A): Treating the entire $40,000 as long-term capital gain ignores the five-year lookback rule, which recharacterizes gains to recapture prior ordinary loss deductions.
Wrong (C): Treating the entire $40,000 as ordinary income overstates the recapture. Only $15,000 of prior losses must be recaptured; the excess retains capital gain treatment.
Wrong (D): This reverses the amounts. The $15,000 is ordinary income (matching the prior losses), not $25,000. The lookback recharacterizes gains equal to the prior losses.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2576;

-- Q2577: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 165(h), the personal-use casualty loss is the lesser of the decline in FMV ($30,000 - $18,000 = $12,000, but FMV was $18,000 pre-storm and car destroyed, so decline = $18,000) or adjusted basis ($30,000), minus insurance proceeds ($15,000). Using the lesser amount ($18,000) minus $15,000 = $3,000 before the $100 floor and 10% AGI limitation.
Wrong (A): $15,000 equals the insurance proceeds, not the casualty loss. The loss is the unreimbursed portion of the lesser of FMV decline or basis.
Wrong (B): $12,000 may incorrectly compute the decline as cost minus FMV ($30,000 - $18,000) without subtracting insurance proceeds.
Wrong (D): $0 would apply only if insurance fully covered the loss. Here, insurance ($15,000) is less than the FMV ($18,000), leaving a $3,000 unreimbursed loss.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2577;

-- Q2578: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 165(g), a loss from worthless securities is treated as a capital loss deemed to occur on the last day of the tax year (December 31, 2026). The holding period is measured to that deemed date, and if held more than one year, the loss is long-term. The taxpayer need not sell the stock to recognize the loss.
Wrong (A): Worthless stock losses are capital losses under Section 165(g), not ordinary losses, unless the stock qualifies under Section 165(g)(3) for certain affiliates.
Wrong (C): The loss is deemed to occur on the last day of the tax year, not the date of the bankruptcy filing. This deemed-date rule can affect the short-term vs. long-term classification.
Wrong (D): Section 165(g) does not require formal cancellation of the stock. The loss is recognized when the stock becomes worthless, regardless of whether shares are formally retired.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2578;

-- Q2579: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the installment method (Section 453), gain is recognized proportionally as payments are received. The gross profit percentage is ($100,000 - $60,000) / $100,000 = 40%. In year one, Ryan recognizes 40% x $30,000 = $12,000 of gain. The installment method spreads gain recognition over the collection period.
Wrong (A): $40,000 is the total gain on the sale, not the gain recognized in year one. Under the installment method, only the proportional share of each payment is recognized.
Wrong (C): $30,000 is the total payment received in year one, not the gain recognized. Only the gross profit percentage (40%) of each payment is gain.
Wrong (D): $0 is incorrect because gain is recognized proportionally as payments are received under Section 453, not deferred entirely until full payment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2579;

-- Q2581: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Kelly''s adjusted basis is $80,000 - $40,000 = $40,000, and her gain is $50,000 - $40,000 = $10,000. Under Section 1245, gain on the sale of depreciable personal property is recaptured as ordinary income to the extent of prior depreciation. Since $10,000 is within the $40,000 of depreciation claimed, all $10,000 is ordinary income.
Wrong (A): Section 1245 recapture takes priority over Section 1231. The gain cannot be treated as Section 1231 gain until depreciation is fully recaptured.
Wrong (C): A $30,000 loss incorrectly computes the loss as $50,000 - $80,000, ignoring the $40,000 depreciation that reduced adjusted basis to $40,000.
Wrong (D): Long-term capital gain treatment is available only for gain exceeding the depreciation recapture amount. Here, the entire $10,000 gain is within the recapture amount.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2581;

-- Q2582: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1211(b), individual taxpayers may deduct a maximum of $3,000 of net capital losses against ordinary income per year ($1,500 if married filing separately). Jennifer deducts $3,000 of her $4,000 loss in 2026, and the remaining $1,000 carries forward under Section 1212(b) as a long-term capital loss.
Wrong (A): $4,000 exceeds the annual $3,000 capital loss deduction limit. Capital losses cannot be fully deducted in the year incurred if they exceed $3,000.
Wrong (B): $0 is incorrect because Section 1211(b) permits up to $3,000 of capital losses to offset ordinary income, even with no capital gains.
Wrong (C): $1,000 is the carryforward amount, not the current-year deduction. The deduction is the lesser of the net loss ($4,000) or the $3,000 limit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2582;

-- Q2583: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): For business property, the casualty gain or loss equals insurance proceeds minus adjusted basis: $310,000 - $200,000 = $110,000 gain. Business casualty gains on Section 1231 property are treated as Section 1231 gains, not subject to the personal-use $100 floor or 10% AGI threshold. This gain may be deferred under Section 1033 if reinvested in qualified replacement property.
Wrong (A): A $40,000 loss incorrectly computes FMV ($350,000) minus insurance ($310,000). Business casualty gains compare insurance proceeds to adjusted basis, not FMV.
Wrong (C): A $150,000 ordinary loss incorrectly uses FMV minus adjusted basis ($350,000 - $200,000) and mischaracterizes the result as a loss when insurance exceeded basis.
Wrong (D): While $110,000 is the correct gain amount, it is properly classified as a Section 1231 gain, not simply a "casualty gain." Section 1231 treatment determines the ultimate character.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2583;
